# 5. CORE GAMEPLAY MECHANICS

## A. Starting the Match

1. All players enter the same lobby.
2. Players spawn at different positions around the room.
3. A countdown appears: 3, 2, 1, GO!
4. The belt is available on the central pedestal.
5. Players rush towards it.
6. The first player to pick it up becomes the Hunter.

Display:

"WHO WILL GET THE BELT?"

When someone collects it, show:

"WE HAVE A HUNTER!"

## B. LIGHTS ON — HUNT PHASE

During this phase:

- The room lights are fully ON.
- The Hunter holds the belt.
- The Hunter can chase other players.
- Survivors must escape, dodge, jump, and hide.
- The Hunter can swing the belt to tag players.

CONTROLS:

Left mouse button = Swing belt.

The belt attack should have:

- Short wind-up animation.
- Swing animation.
- Fair hit detection.
- Cooldown after swinging.
- Funny sound effect.

When a player is hit:

- Play a funny stumbling animation.
- Briefly stun the player.
- Add a comic impact effect.
- Display "YOU GOT TAGGED!"

Avoid realistic violence.

A tagged player should be temporarily stunned rather than permanently eliminated in the initial version.

## C. BLACKOUT — REVERSE HUNT PHASE

THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT UNIQUE MECHANIC.

At random intervals, the room lights switch OFF.

The game should transition into a blackout phase.

During blackout:

- Main room lighting goes dark.
- Hunter loses most of their visibility.
- Hunter can still move.
- Survivors can move freely.
- Survivors can approach the Hunter.
- Survivors can attempt to tag the Hunter and steal the belt.

BELT STEAL MECHANIC:

A survivor must get close to the Hunter and hold E for approximately 1 second.

If the interaction succeeds:

- The belt transfers to that player.
- The original Hunter loses the belt.
- A belt-steal sound plays.
- Display a notification.

"BELT STOLEN!"

Only ONE successful belt steal is allowed per blackout.

If nobody steals the belt, the original Hunter keeps it.

IMPORTANT:
Do not reveal the identity of the new Hunter immediately.

Keep the room slightly visible during blackout so players can navigate.

Do not make the screen completely black.

## D. LIGHTS RETURN

Before the lights return:

- Play a warning buzzer.
- Flicker the lights.
- Give players a final 2-second warning.

When lights turn ON:

- Reveal the current belt-holder.
- The belt-holder becomes the Hunter.
- Display a large animated notification:

"NEW HUNTER!"

If the original Hunter still has the belt, they continue hunting.

# 6. LIGHT CYCLE

Start with these values:

- Lights ON: 12 seconds.
- Lights OFF: 10 seconds.
- Warning before lights return: 2 seconds.
- Match duration: 3–5 minutes.

Add small random variations to the cycle.

The timing must be controlled by the server and synchronized across all players.

# 7. TAGGING AND PLAYER STATES

Implement a clean player state system.

Possible states:

- NORMAL
- HUNTER
- STUNNED
- STEALING
- SPECTATING
- DISCONNECTED

The server should control all important player states.

The Hunter can tag survivors only during Lights ON.

Survivors can steal the belt only during BLACKOUT.

Prevent players from abusing the attack and steal buttons.

# 8. WINNING CONDITIONS

Recommended initial rules:

HUNTER WINS:
The Hunter successfully tags all other active players before the match timer ends.

SURVIVORS WIN:
At least one survivor remains uncaught until the match timer expires.

Keep players involved as much as possible.

Do not introduce permanent elimination in the first prototype.

