# 12. MULTIPLAYER REQUIREMENTS

This MUST be genuine multiplayer.

Target: 4–10 real players in one room.

Do not create fake multiplayer by spawning locally controlled duplicate characters.

Use an authoritative server architecture.

The server must control:

- Player connections.
- Player spawning.
- Belt ownership.
- Hunter identity.
- Lights ON/OFF state.
- Phase timers.
- Belt attacks.
- Hit detection validation.
- Belt stealing.
- Player stun states.
- Match results.

All clients must receive synchronized updates.

When one player steals the belt, every other player must see the ownership change.

When the lights turn OFF, they must turn OFF for everyone at the same time.

When the lights return, everyone must see the same Hunter.

Handle disconnects without breaking the match.

If the project is currently using a local-only setup, implement a proper networking solution rather than pretending multiplayer works.

# 13. TECHNICAL STACK

First inspect the existing project.

If the project is empty, choose one consistent architecture.

For a browser-based 3D game, a possible stack is:

- React.
- Three.js / React Three Fiber.
- Rapier physics.
- An authoritative multiplayer server.
- WebSocket-based state synchronization or an appropriate multiplayer framework.

Alternatively, use Godot if the project is better suited to a standalone game.

Do not combine unrelated engines.

Choose a stack based on the actual project and explain the decision briefly.

